+
+# Decision: Concurrency Policy for Independent Edits to One Visit
+
+## Context
+
+Field workers on shared tablets sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. When two edits to one visit arrive at the record store, each derived from a different starting state of that visit, the store must decide what the visit's current record becomes. That policy is load-bearing for the rest of the system: it determines what tablets must store and submit, what review work coordinators see, and which record states can enter the partner's weekly CSV. Getting it wrong either silently loses a worker's submitted observations or buries two coordinators under review volume they cannot clear before the Friday export.
+
+Two properties bound every option here. The server must not treat arrival order as evidence that one submission is more authoritative. And no submitted edit may be silently discarded — any mechanism that overwrites or drops one worker's payload in favor of another fails before scoring begins.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No silent loss** *(inherited)*: the mechanism must preserve every accepted submission's payload in full, regardless of outcome.
+- **No arrival-order authority** *(inherited)*: the mechanism must not make the later-arriving or earlier-arriving edit current merely because of its position in the submission queue.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Scientific integrity of automated combination** *(major; tenet: "Do not silently discard a submitted edit")*: where a mechanism combines edits automatically, it must not combine field changes that carry a scientific relationship the server cannot see — a revised species identification and its cover estimate, for example. A combination that is textually valid but scientifically incoherent is a silent corruption of the record.
+- **Review workload** *(major; tenet: "Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator")*: the mechanism must produce review volume two coordinators can clear on a ~6,000-visit season with a Friday deadline.
+- **Explainability of the exported record** *(moderate; tenet: "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable")*: the mechanism must make it traceable which submitted observation a recorded value came from.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** *(moderate)*: rules the team must write and keep correct (merge logic, field taxonomies) are a standing cost and a standing defect surface.
+- **Offline durability of the tablet edit** *(minor)*: what a tablet must retain per pending edit affects local storage and the complexity of the retry state machine.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+Each tablet edit names the visit revision it started from and submits the complete visit payload. The server appends the submission as a new revision of the visit. When the base revision is still the visit's current tip, the edit simply extends it. When two edits share an earlier base, they become competing whole-visit revisions, and the visit is flagged unresolved until a coordinator designates one as the current record. Nothing is ever merged automatically: the server's job is to keep every revision intact and make the competition visible.
+
+*Demonstration:* Workers A and B both fetch revision 4 of visit `9f2c…`. A corrects an observer name and submits, creating revision 5. Offline, B adds a missing species and submits, also based on revision 4. The server appends B's payload as a competing revision, marks the visit unresolved, and both complete payloads sit side by side for a coordinator, each showing its author and receipt time.
+
+*Analysis:*
+
+- No silent loss: **passes** — revisions are append-only; losing revisions remain in the chain as history and provenance.
+- No arrival-order authority: **passes** — whichever edit arrives first becomes the interim tip only as the base for comparison; the coordinator's designation, not arrival position, sets the current record.
+- Scientific integrity: **strong** — no automated combination exists, so no scientific relationship can be crossed without a person in the loop.
+- Review workload: **weak** — every concurrent edit pair creates review work, including edits to unrelated fields (an observer-name correction colliding with a new species row).
+- Explainability: **strong** — an exported value is the payload of one identified, authored revision; provenance is whole-record.
+- Implementation burden: **strong** — the server stores and orders revisions; it maintains no merge rules.
+- Offline durability: **partial** — a tablet retains the base revision ID per pending edit and must resend the full payload, but needs no local snapshot diffing.
+
+*Summary:* trades review volume for absolute preservation and near-zero maintained logic.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+Each tablet edit supplies the base state it started from and a structured set of field-level changes. The server applies each change against the base and, when concurrent edits touch disjoint fields, combines them automatically into a new current revision. Only edits that touch the same field collide, and those become review work. The server maintains typed merge rules — overwriting scalars, unioning observer lists, merging species entries by taxon — and records field-level provenance so each value in the current record traces to a contributing submission.
+
+*Demonstration:* Workers A and B both start from revision 4. A's change set alters the observer list; B's adds a species row. The sets are disjoint, so the server applies both to the base and records the result as the current revision, with per-field provenance pointing at A for the observer fields and B for the species row. Had both touched the species *Carex arenaria* entry, that field would be routed to review.
+
+*Analysis:*
+
+- No silent loss: **passes** — the original submissions are retained; combination produces a derived record, not a replacement.
+- No arrival-order authority: **passes** — combination is keyed on which fields changed, not on arrival sequence.
+- Scientific integrity: **weak** — the merge rules operate on field names, and the server cannot know which fields carry a scientific relationship. Disjoint-by-field changes can be scientifically entangled (a revised identification and its cover estimate live in different fields), so an automatic combination can be coherent structurally and wrong botanically, with nobody notified.
+- Review workload: **strong** — only overlapping-field collisions reach review; most concurrent edits resolve automatically.
+- Explainability: **partial** — provenance exists but is field-granular against a derived record; the exported value is assembled, not authored.
+- Implementation burden: **weak** — typed merge rules for scalars, lists, and species entries must be written, kept correct, and extended as the payload schema grows.
+- Offline durability: **weak** — the tablet retains base snapshots and computes structured change sets, complicating the retry state machine.
+
+*Summary:* trades maintained merge logic and scientific-relationship risk for a much smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** The decision turns on the scientific-integrity criterion, weighted major and anchored to the no-silent-loss tenet. The merge option's central benefit — resolving most concurrent edits without a person — is also its central risk: automatically combining unrelated-looking fields can cross exactly the scientific relationships the team would least want silently resolved, and the server has no basis for knowing which those are. Whole-visit revisions put every concurrent edit in front of a person at whole-record granularity, which is explainable and honest about what the server does not know.
+
+The review-workload cost is real but survivable at this scale: concurrent corrections to the same visit are occasional, two coordinators exist for exactly this resolution work, and the alternative's cost is a standing rulebase whose failure mode is silent. The implementation-burden criterion points the same direction. The decision does not turn on the offline-durability criterion (minor either way); it is recorded because the losing option carries most of that cost.
+
+**When this would be re-litigated:** the merge option wins if review volume from concurrent edits grows enough to routinely delay the Friday export — the criterion this decision knowingly concedes. Concretely: if coordinators cannot clear the unresolved queue within a normal week at observed concurrency rates, the trade should be revisited, ideally scoping automatic combination to field groups the team has reviewed and agreed are scientifically independent.
+
+**Committing obligates downstream work:**
+
+- *Tablet-sync* must retain, per pending edit, the base revision number the edit started from; submit complete visit payloads rather than deltas; keep idempotency keys per pending edit across retries; present to field workers when their edit has become a pending competing revision and what the coordinator resolved; and re-base any post-resolution edit on the resolved revision.
+- *Review-export* must provide a comparison view over competing whole-visit revisions (author, receipt time, base, payload); exclude unresolved visits from the export set; and record export provenance at revision granularity — an exported value is the payload of one designated revision, never a server-assembled combination.
